Weak passwords are one of the main reasons people get hacked. Many users still choose simple or repeated passwords because they want something easy to remember. This makes it easy for attackers to guess or steal accounts.

A strong password is usually long, unique, and not based on personal information. Research recommends using passphrases (like BlueDoorRunsFast!), mixing characters, and avoiding the same password on different accounts. Turning on two‑factor authentication adds extra protection.

Common attacks include brute‑force guessing, dictionary attacks using common passwords, and credential‑stuffing where hackers try leaked passwords on other sites. These attacks work mainly because people reuse weak passwords.

Teenagers and students are especially at risk because they manage many accounts and often prioritise convenience. Your game helps fix this by teaching good habits through simple choices, instant feedback, and realistic scenarios.

The goal is to help players learn how to create safer passwords and understand why strong password behaviour matters in everyday life.
